Configuration
*************

.[perex]
How to wire Butler into an application in Nette and without it, where it stores pending work and what you can configure.


Nette
=====

The extension registers the store, `Butler\Runner` and `Butler\Cli` in the DI container:

```neon
extensions:
	butler: Butler\Bridges\Nette\ButlerExtension

butler:
	production: true
```

Tasks have to be services in the container, Butler takes them from there by their class. The simplest way is to have them registered automatically:

```neon
search:
	tasks:
		in: %appDir%/Tasks
		implements:
			- Butler\Task
			- Butler\PayloadTask
```


production
----------

Says whether the application runs in production. Outside of it, Butler runs only tasks with the attribute [#[Butler\AllowedLocally] |tasks#Tasks That Run Only in Production]. The option is mandatory, so that it cannot be forgotten.

Put the value into a configuration that differs between production and your machine, for example `production: true` into the common configuration and `production: false` into the local one, which only a developer's machine loads.

Do not use `%productionMode%` from Nette. It is `false` in the console and for a developer's IP address, so on the production server it would forbid the tasks exactly in cron.


dir
---

The directory where Butler writes pending work and locks. The default is `%tempDir%/butler`.

The directory must survive deploying a new version and must not be emptied together with the cache. Emptying `temp/cache` does not bother it, emptying the whole `temp` does, because the recorded and not yet processed work would disappear. Both PHP-FPM and cron must be able to write into it, that is both users they run as.

When two applications share one `temp` and have tasks of the same class, give each its own directory. Otherwise a run of one application would merge with a run of the other.


maxQueueSize
------------

How many payloads can wait in the queue of one `PayloadTask` at most. The default is 1000. The endpoint refuses the next request with code 503, so flooding a public action does not fill the disk.


maxAttempts
-----------

How many times Butler tries to process a payload before it [rejects |how-it-works#Rejected Payloads] it. The default is 10.


staleAfter
----------

After how many seconds of waiting the command [drain |cli#Cron: drain] reports work as stuck. The default is 3600, which is an hour.


onRun
-----

A handler that gets a report about every run of a task, successful or not. The default one writes failures to the Tracy log, and to `error_log` when Tracy is not installed. You set your own handler like this:

```neon
butler:
	onRun: App\Monitoring::recordRun(...)
```

```php
final class Monitoring
{
	public static function recordRun(Butler\RunReport $report): void
	{
		// $report->task      class of the task
		// $report->entry     the work processed, null when the task failed before it
		// $report->duration  duration of the run in seconds
		// $report->error     the exception, or null on success
	}
}
```


Without Nette
=============

`Butler\Runner` needs four things: a store, a factory of tasks, the environment and a handler of runs.

```php
$runner = new Butler\Runner(
	store: new Butler\FileStore(__DIR__ . '/data/butler'),
	factory: fn(string $task) => new $task,
	production: getenv('APP_ENV') === 'production',
	onRun: function (Butler\RunReport $report): void {
		if ($report->error) {
			error_log((string) $report->error);
		}
	},
);
```

The factory gets the class of a task and returns its instance. When tasks need dependencies, create them here, or take them from the container you use.

`FileStore` has two more optional parameters, `maxQueueSize` and `maxAttempts`, with the same meaning as the options above.

